        "body": "> By the way I just realized with this, new contributors do not even have to download anything locally to start working on Node.js, they can just use GitHub codespace to spin off the developement environment in the browser. This may be very handy for future code and learn sessions - just click a few buttons and you can start working on Node.js core. \r\n\r\nYeah! This was the original intent. The one unfortunate thing that prevented this from being an easy reality is that because of how billing works for Codespaces, there's not really a way for our org to absorb the cost - so the cost of spinning up that Codespaces instance would be on the end-user, and IIRC the base free machine doesn't cover the minimum needs for this devcontainer in a way that is cheap. But, for anyone in the org they should be able to get it for free.",
